"""
app.py
=======
Entry point for Phase 1 of the AR-Based Gesture Controlled System.

Responsibilities:
  1. Initialize logging + load config.
  2. Open the webcam via the Camera module (as a context manager, so it
     is guaranteed to be released even if something crashes).
  3. Run every captured frame through HandTracker to get landmarks +
     an annotated preview frame.
  4. Overlay FPS and hand-count text.
  5. Handle keyboard controls (quit, screenshot, toggle overlays).
  6. Guarantee clean shutdown of camera + MediaPipe resources on exit,
     whether that exit is normal (user pressed 'q') or an exception.

Run with:   python app.py
Quit with:  'q' key, or close the OpenCV window


"""
config.py
==========
Centralized configuration for the AR-Based Gesture Controlled System.

Design rationale:
- All tunable parameters live in ONE place so that every module (camera,
  hand tracker, app loop) reads consistent values instead of hardcoding
  magic numbers across the codebase. This is critical for an "industrial
  quality" project: when Phase 2 adds gesture classification or a PyQt5
  GUI, those modules will import the SAME config object and never drift
  out of sync with camera/tracker settings.
- Python's `dataclass` gives us type hints, default values, and easy
  extensibility without boilerplate __init__ methods.
- A single module-level `config` instance is created at the bottom of
  this file and imported everywhere else (`from config import config`).
  This is the standard "singleton via module" pattern in Python - since
  modules are only ever imported once and cached in sys.modules, every
  file that imports `config` gets the exact same object in memory.
"""

"""
modules/hand_tracker.py
=========================
Wraps Google's MediaPipe Hands solution to provide:
  - multi-hand detection (Left / Right / multiple hands simultaneously)
  - 21-landmark extraction per hand, in BOTH pixel and normalized coords
  - a padded bounding box per hand
  - skeleton + label + landmark-ID drawing on the output frame

This module is the "brain" of Phase 1. Every other module (camera, fps,
utils) exists to feed frames into this module and display its output.
Phase 2 (gesture classification) will consume the `HandData` objects this
module returns without needing to touch MediaPipe directly at all.
"""
"""
modules/fps.py
===============
FPS (Frames Per Second) measurement utility.

Why a dedicated module?
- FPS logic is needed for on-screen display NOW, and will be needed for
  performance logging/analytics dashboards in later phases. Isolating it
  here avoids duplicating timing code inside app.py or hand_tracker.py.

Approach:
- Uses an Exponential Moving Average (EMA) instead of a raw frame-to-frame
  delta, so the displayed number doesn't jitter wildly between frames
  (e.g. 28 -> 45 -> 22 -> 40). This gives a stable, readable FPS counter.
"""
"""
modules/camera.py
===================
Wraps cv2.VideoCapture with:
  - robust initialization + error handling (raises a clear, catchable
    exception instead of silently returning black frames)
  - configurable resolution / target FPS / buffer size
  - context-manager support (`with Camera(...) as cam:`) for guaranteed
    resource cleanup even if an exception occurs mid-loop
  - structured logging of every lifecycle event

Isolating camera logic here means app.py - and any future module such as
a PyQt5 GUI in Phase 2 - can reuse this exact, tested camera interface
instead of re-implementing raw cv2.VideoCapture calls in multiple places.
"""


